# Healenium Annotations – Detailed Study Notes

Java | Selenium WebDriver | TestNG | Page Object Model (POM)

## 1. Introduction

Healenium is an open-source, self-healing automation library that integrates with Selenium WebDriver to reduce test failures caused by changes in UI element locators.

In traditional Selenium automation, if an element's ID, XPath, or CSS selector changes, the test may fail with a `NoSuchElementException`.

Healenium attempts to identify an alternative locator using previously captured element information and similarity-based matching.

Healenium annotations are used to control or enhance certain element-identification and self-healing behaviors. However, not all annotations used in a Healenium framework belong to Healenium itself.

### Annotation categories

| Category             | Examples                                          |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Healenium-specific   | `@DisableHealing`, `@PageAwareFindBy`             |
| Selenium PageFactory | `@FindBy`, `@FindBys`, `@FindAll`, `@CacheLookup` |
| TestNG lifecycle     | `@Test`, `@BeforeMethod`, `@AfterMethod`          |
| Reporting (Allure)   | `@Step`, `@Description`, `@Severity`              |

The following notes focus on the Healenium-specific annotations and their practical use in automation testing.

## 2. `@DisableHealing`

### Definition

`@DisableHealing` is a Healenium annotation used to temporarily disable the self-healing mechanism for an annotated method.

This is useful when the test must validate the actual existence or absence of an element without Healenium substituting a similar element.

### Why is it required?

Consider a login page where the application originally contains:

```
<button id="loginButton">Login</button>
```

After a UI change, the original element is removed and another button is introduced:

```
<button id="submitButton">Submit</button>
```

Under suitable conditions, Healenium may identify the new button as a replacement for the old one.

However, when testing whether the original login button has been removed, automatic healing could produce a misleading result.

Using `@DisableHealing` allows you to test the absence of the original locator without automatic recovery.

### Java example

```
import com.epam.healenium.annotation.DisableHealing;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.NoSuchElementException;

public class LoginPage {

    @DisableHealing
    public boolean isOldLoginButtonPresent() {
        try {
            return driver.findElement(
                By.id("loginButton")
            ).isDisplayed();
        } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

### Key characteristics

- Disables healing for supported annotated method executions.
- Useful for negative testing and UI removal validation.
- Does not permanently disable healing for the entire framework.
- Requires a compatible Healenium-Web version and invocation setup.
- Does not prevent Selenium exceptions unrelated to locator healing.

### Practical testing scenarios

| Scenario                    | Why disable healing?                           |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Verify removed UI elements  | Ensure an obsolete element is genuinely absent |
| Negative login testing      | Avoid substituting an unintended element       |
| Validate deprecated buttons | Confirm old functionality is removed           |
| Validate locator changes    | Observe the original locator failure           |
| Debug self-healing          | Compare behavior with and without healing      |

Important: If your test specifically needs to verify that a locator is broken, disabling healing is appropriate. If the objective is to validate that the application workflow survives a locator change, leave healing enabled.

## 3. `@PageAwareFindBy`

### Definition

`@PageAwareFindBy` is a Healenium annotation that extends Selenium's `@FindBy` functionality by associating a locator with a specific page.

It helps Healenium distinguish between similar locators appearing on different pages or application states.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://deepwiki.com\&sz=32)

DeepWiki+1



### Syntax

```
@PageAwareFindBy(
    page = "LoginPage",
    findBy = @FindBy(id = "username")
)
private WebElement usernameField;
```

### Parameters

| Parameter | Description                                     |
| --------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| `page`    | Logical name of the page containing the element |
| `findBy`  | Selenium `@FindBy` locator definition           |

If `page` is omitted, the declaring class name is used as the default page context.

### Example: Kiosk Labour Login

```
import com.epam.healenium.annotation.PageAwareFindBy;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.FindBy;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.PageFactory;

public class KioskLabourLoginPage {

    @PageAwareFindBy(
        page = "KioskLabourLogin",
        findBy = @FindBy(id = "mobileNumber")
    )
    private WebElement mobileNumber;

    @PageAwareFindBy(
        page = "KioskLabourLogin",
        findBy = @FindBy(id = "sendOTP")
    )
    private WebElement sendOTP;

    public KioskLabourLoginPage(
            WebDriver driver) {
        PageFactory.initElements(driver, this);
    }

    public void enterMobileNumber(String number) {
        mobileNumber.sendKeys(number);
    }

    public void clickSendOTP() {
        sendOTP.click();
    }
}
```

The IDs in this example are illustrative and must be replaced with actual application locators.

### How it works

`@PageAwareFindBy`

Defines locator and page context

PageFactory initialization

Processes annotated element fields

Page-aware locator

Associates the element with a logical page

Healenium locator recovery

Uses the context when identifying possible replacement elements

### When to use it

- Applications with multiple pages containing similar buttons.
- Kiosk applications where the same field IDs appear across modules.
- Large Page Object Model frameworks.
- Applications with reusable UI components.
- Situations where healing must distinguish between similar elements.

## 4. Difference between `@FindBy` and `@PageAwareFindBy`

| Feature                              | `@FindBy` | `@PageAwareFindBy`            |
| ------------------------------------ | --------- | ----------------------------- |
| Library                              | Selenium  | Healenium                     |
| Identifies elements                  | Yes       | Yes                           |
| Supports XPath, ID, CSS              | Yes       | Yes, through nested `@FindBy` |
| Adds page context                    | No        | Yes                           |
| Compatible with POM                  | Yes       | Yes                           |
| Supports self-healing with Healenium | Yes       | Yes                           |
| Requires page name                   | No        | Optional                      |

Important: You do not need to replace all existing `@FindBy` annotations with `@PageAwareFindBy`. Standard Selenium locators already work with Healenium-Web when properly configured.&#x20;

